// =============================================================================
// MemQL Action Reference
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for the `action` syntax. It is NOT loaded
// by the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to skip
// it -- see component/memql/dslfs/walker.go).
//
// `action` is the behavioral DSL's EXTERNAL-SIDE-EFFECT construct. From the
// behavioral-constructs ADR (docs/internal/design/dsl-behavioral-constructs-adr.md,
// §2 + §2.3):
//
//     logic decides, mutations persist, actions touch the world,
//     queries read, automations orchestrate and react.
//
// An action performs EXACTLY ONE external capability call on a surface
// (shell.* / fs.* / http.* / integration.* / mcp.*) and NEVER touches the
// MemQL graph. The dividing line is "what does it touch?": the outside world
// -> action; our graph -> mutation; read/compute -> query / logic. Because an
// action's effect is reproducible from its inputs, it can be replayed and
// fingerprint-verified token-free -- the foundation the action library and the
// deployment bundle are built on.
//
// An authored action is DECLARATIVE, not procedural: it names a capability,
// declares its input `params`, and renders an `argTemplate` from those params.
// At run time an automation step `action("name@1") { args { ... } }` binds the
// step args to the action's params, renders the argTemplate, and invokes the
// single capability on the resolved surface. There is no body, no control flow,
// and no graph access -- that is what keeps an action atomic and replayable.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Call-graph contract (ADR §2 table) -- enforced by the call-graph validator (I7)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Construct | May call                                  | May NOT
//   ----------|-------------------------------------------|--------------------
//   action    | exactly ONE external capability on a      | call logic / query /
//             | resolved surface (shell.* / fs.* /        | mutation / automation;
//             | http.* / integration.* / mcp.*)           | touch the graph; carry
//             |                                           | a @trigger; >1 capability
//
// The action rules (one external capability; no calls into logic / query /
// mutation / automation; no graph access) are enforced by the I7 call-graph
// validator alongside the capability vocabulary. An action cannot self-persist
// its result -- it returns a value and the NEXT automation step is a mutation
// that writes it (the decide -> act -> persist rhythm; ADR §2.3).
//
// =============================================================================
//   1. Action-level annotations
// =============================================================================
//
// Every annotation goes above the `action` keyword.
//
//   @description("text")   Human-readable description.                 optional
//   @kind("primitive")     Action kind. PRIMITIVE = one capability call
//                          rendered from params (the only kind today). The
//                          composite kind from the action-library draft is
//                          retired -- a "composite" is just an automation that
//                          has been promoted into the library (ADR §2.2).   @kind("primitive")
//   @sideEffect("read"|"write"|"exec")
//                          Coarse risk class carried on the action for
//                          authoring + the surface-aware trust gate. The
//                          AUTHORITATIVE sideEffectClass lives on the
//                          CAPABILITY (ADR §7) so it cannot be spoofed by an
//                          authored or generated action; the I7 capability
//                          vocabulary reconciles the two.                  optional
//   @enabled / @disabled   Lifecycle flag (shared across constructs). @enabled
//                          is the explicit-on default; @disabled skips the
//                          action at load.                                optional
//
// NOTE: an action never carries a @trigger -- reactivity is automations-only
// (ADR §2.4). An action is reached by an automation step, never by an event.
//
// =============================================================================
//   2. Anatomy -- capability + intent + params + argTemplate
// =============================================================================
//
// An action body has four parts, all flat (no procedural statements):
//
//   capability "<namespace>.<verb>"   The single external capability this
//                                     action performs. Required. One per
//                                     action -- the validator rejects a second.
//   intent "<text>"                   Natural-language description of what the
//                                     action does -- the future embedding
//                                     source for planner retrieval. Required.
//   params { <name> <type> [@required] [@description("...")] ... }
//                                     The action's input schema. Field syntax
//                                     mirrors every other construct's args.
//   argTemplate { <key>: "<template>" ... }
//                                     The concrete capability arguments,
//                                     rendered from params. A "$params.<name>"
//                                     reference is substituted with the bound
//                                     param value before the capability runs.
//
// Compliant example -- the deployment bundle's clone primitive (ADR §4):

// A `shell.*` action runs a NAMED capability script (the I14 contract, #2221),
// NOT an arbitrary command string. The reserved `script` arg names the
// allowlisted capability-script id; every other arg is a structured param. The
// action executor's capability-script runner
// (component/automations/steps/capability_script.go) resolves the id to a
// scripts/* path, runs it with the params as `--name=value` argv flags (no
// shell, no `sh -c`), and parses the one JSON envelope it writes to stdout.
// This is injection-safe by construction -- an author/event value can only pick
// an allowlisted script and travels as data, never as a command line. (The raw
// `shell.exec` + `cmd:` rendered-command form is RETIRED for exactly that
// reason -- it was a command-injection sink.)

@kind("primitive")
@sideEffect("exec")
@description("Check out a git repository at a specific ref on the deploy runner.")
action cloneRepoAtVersion {
  capability "shell.script"
  intent "Clone/checkout a repository working tree at the requested ref so a build runs against a pinned version."
  params {
    workdir string @required @description("Absolute path of the repository working tree on the surface.")
    ref     string @required @description("Git ref (branch, tag, or SHA) to check out.")
  }
  argTemplate {
    script:  "deploy.cloneRepo"
    workdir: "$params.workdir"
    ref:     "$params.ref"
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   3. Other capability namespaces -- fs / http / integration
// =============================================================================
//
// The capability namespace selects the backend; the shape is identical.
//
// 3a. A filesystem read (sideEffect read):

@kind("primitive")
@sideEffect("read")
@description("Read a file from the surface filesystem.")
action readManifest {
  capability "fs.readFile"
  intent "Read a deployment manifest off the surface so a later step can inspect it."
  params {
    path string @required @description("Absolute path to read.")
  }
  argTemplate {
    path: "$params.path"
  }
}

// 3b. An integration-backed action. A SIDE-EFFECTING integration (e.g. tag a
//     release, post to Slack) is reachable ONLY through an action -- its
//     capability resolves to integration.* instead of shell.*/fs.* (ADR §3).
//     A read-only integration stays a builtin callable from queries/logic.

@kind("primitive")
@sideEffect("write")
@description("Tag a GitHub release for a shipped version.")
action tagRelease {
  capability "integration.github.tagRelease"
  intent "Cut a git tag + release on GitHub for the version that just deployed."
  params {
    repo    string @required @description("owner/name of the repository.")
    version string @required @description("Semver tag to create, e.g. 0.12.0.")
  }
  argTemplate {
    repo: "$params.repo"
    tag:  "$params.version"
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   4. Calling an action -- the automation step (ADR §7)
// =============================================================================
//
// An action is invoked from an automation step by its pinned-by-default
// reference "name@version". Step args bind to the action's params; omitting
// `on surface(...)` defers surface selection to the resolver:
//
//   use deployment.actions.{ cloneRepoAtVersion }
//
//   automation deployEngine @trigger(event="deploy.requested.staging") {
//     step clone {
//       action("cloneRepoAtVersion@1") {
//         args { workdir: "/work/memql", ref: event.payload.ref }
//       }
//     }
//     // ... next step is a mutation that records the result (decide->act->persist)
//   }
//
// An identical input replays token-free: binding the same args renders the
// same argTemplate, the capability runs on the default surface, and the
// result fingerprint (component/harness/actionreplay.Fingerprint) reproduces.
// A mismatch is what the surface-aware trust ladder escalates on (later phase).
//
// =============================================================================
//   5. NON-COMPLIANT examples (rejected by the I7 call-graph validator)
// =============================================================================
//
// 5a. An action that calls into another construct. An action is atomic: it may
//     not call a logic / query / mutation / automation.
//
//   // INVALID: action calls a logic (must be a single external capability).
//   action deployBad {
//     capability "shell.exec"
//     intent "..."
//     params { ref string @required }
//     argTemplate { cmd: nextSemver(args.ref) }   // <- calls logic: rejected
//   }
//
// 5b. An action that touches the graph. Graph writes stay mutations; an action
//     never reaches MemQL.
//
//   // INVALID: an action may not perform a graph write -- that is a mutation.
//   action recordBad {
//     capability "shell.exec"
//     intent "..."
//     params { id string @required }
//     argTemplate { cmd: "createDeployment({ id: $params.id })" }  // <- graph write: rejected
//   }
//
// 5c. An action declaring more than one capability. Exactly one external
//     capability per action (ADR §2.3); compose multiples in an automation.
//
//   // INVALID: two capabilities in one action.
//   action twoThingsBad {
//     capability "shell.exec"
//     capability "fs.writeFile"   // <- second capability: rejected
//     intent "..."
//     params { }
//     argTemplate { }
//   }
//
// 5d. An action carrying a trigger. Reactivity is automations-only (ADR §2.4).
//
//   // INVALID: only automations may carry @trigger.
//   @trigger(event="deploy.requested")   // <- rejected on an action
//   action onDeployBad { capability "shell.exec" intent "..." params {} argTemplate {} }
//
// See `_automation.memql` for the construct that orchestrates actions, and
// `_logic.memql` for the pure-decision construct an action's inputs come from.
// =============================================================================
